+# Windows ARM64 Port
+
+Native Windows ARM64 (`aarch64`) support for dash 0.5.13.5 as shipped in the
+MSYS2 (`usr/bin`) layer of Git for Windows, on branch `arm64-port`.
+
+dash is a POSIX program built against the MSYS2/Cygwin runtime (`msys-2.0.dll`).
+Its Windows ARM64 target is therefore **`aarch64-pc-cygwin`**, built with the GCC
+cross toolchain. MSVC, MSBuild and CMake do not apply.
+
+## Summary of Changes
+### Build System
+- `configure.ac`, `configure` (regenerated with autoconf 2.72, the version that
+  generated the committed file; 1-line diff each): in the non-cross branch, use
+  `CC_FOR_BUILD=${CC_FOR_BUILD-$CC}` instead of `CC_FOR_BUILD=${CC}`.
+  - The bug: with only `--host=aarch64-pc-cygwin` (no `--build`), autoconf starts
+    with `cross_compiling=maybe`. On a Windows ARM64 machine where the aarch64
+    test program can run, that resolves to `no`, and configure then silently
+    discarded a user-supplied `CC_FOR_BUILD`. The generators (`mkinit`,
+    `mksyntax`, `mknodes`, `mksignames`) were then built as aarch64 binaries,
+    which the x86_64 msys `make` cannot run.
+  - Reproduced on this host with the ARM64 runtime on PATH. Old configure gave
+    `cross compiling... no` / `build system compiler... aarch64-pc-cygwin-gcc`;
+    new configure gives `... no` / `... gcc`.
+  - Native builds that do not set `CC_FOR_BUILD` still get `$CC`. Commit `047daaf`.
+
+### Source Code
+- None. The source has no x86-specific code: no SIMD, no intrinsics, no inline
+  asm other than the empty compiler barrier in `src/error.h:83`, and no
+  `__x86_64__` guards. The only platform guard is `__CYGWIN__` in `src/cd.c`,
+  which aarch64-pc-cygwin also defines.
+- Plain `char` is signed on aarch64-pc-cygwin (`__CHAR_UNSIGNED__` is not
+  defined), the same as x64. The CTL* byte handling uses explicit
+  `signed char` casts anyway.
+
+### CI/CD
+- None. The tree has no CI configuration; ARM64 CI lives in the MSYS2-packages recipe.
+
+### New Files
+- `benchmarks/base_bench_win_arm.txt`, `benchmarks/base_bench_win_arm.meta.json`,
+  `benchmarks/scripts/*.sh`, `benchmarks/scripts/run_bench.ps1` (commit `84c1485`).
+
+## Build Steps (ARM64)
+Prerequisites: MSYS2 with `autotools`, `make` and `gcc` (x86_64-pc-msys, the
+build compiler), and the aarch64-pc-cygwin cross toolchain with its sysroot at
+`/usr/aarch64-pc-cygwin` (GCC 17.0.0 20260828, binutils 2.47.50, ARM64 msys2-runtime).
+
+### Build
+```powershell
+$env:MSYSTEM = 'MSYS'
+C:\msys64\usr\bin\bash -lc 'cd /c/wos-eval/work/dash && mkdir -p build-arm64 && cd build-arm64 &&
+  ../configure --prefix=/usr --exec-prefix= --bindir=/usr/bin --mandir=/usr/share/man \
+    --build=x86_64-pc-msys --host=aarch64-pc-cygwin CC_FOR_BUILD=gcc CFLAGS="-O2 -static-libgcc" &&
+  make'
+```
+Build output: `build-arm64/src/dash.exe`. The generators are built as x86-64
+with `gcc`; `dash.exe` is AA64 and imports only `KERNEL32.dll` and `msys-2.0.dll`.
+
+### Run Tests
+dash has no upstream test suite. Phase 6 used a 45-script differential
+conformance suite. Each script ran under the ARM64 dash (PATH = an ARM64 MSYS
+tree, here `C:\gfw-native\native\usr\bin`) and under the x64 dash (PATH =
+`C:\msys64\usr\bin`), and stdout, stderr and exit code were compared byte for
+byte. Each dash was launched directly from `powershell.exe`, so it is the root
+Cygwin process:
+```powershell
+powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File benchmarks\tmp\run_suite.ps1 -Arch arm64 -Tag run1
+powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File benchmarks\tmp\run_suite.ps1 -Arch x64   -Tag run1
+sh benchmarks/tmp/compare.sh run1
+```
+(The suite is local scratch and is not committed.) Expected: 45 passed, 0 failed.
+
+### Run Benchmarks
+```powershell
+powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File benchmarks\scripts\run_bench.ps1 -Runs 5
+```
+Output: `benchmarks/base_bench_win_arm.txt`.
+
+## Test Results
+- **Build host**: native ARM64 (Snapdragon X Elite X1E80100, Windows 11 26100)
+- **Executed**: Yes, natively, 3 full runs per arch
+- **Passed**: 45     **Failed**: 0     **Skipped**: 0 (in each of the 3 final runs)
+
+Areas covered:
+- parameter expansion and quoting, including UTF-8 and the CTLESC-range bytes 0x81-0x88
+- intmax arithmetic edges, overflow, shifts and error cases
+- here-docs, case/glob, functions/local
+- `set -e` and errors in subshells (setjmp/longjmp paths)
+- EXIT/INT/USR1/USR2/HUP/TERM traps, `kill -l` (mksignames output)
+- `&`/`wait` including a 40-job stress test, and signalled-child exit codes
+- read/printf/test/getopts, redirections, pipelines, nested command substitution
+- `cd -P`/`pwd` with Windows paths (cygwin_conv_path)
+
+The first pass had 2-3 mismatches. None was a port defect:
+- `t08_arith_errors`, `t23_test_builtin`: harness artifacts (the shell's own
+  path and `$$` in the output). Normalised in the test scripts.
+- `t20_signalled_exit` (1 of 3 runs): `sleep 5 & kill -INT $!` sometimes
+  gives 130. This is an upstream dash race, not ARM64-specific: `forkchild()`
+  (`src/jobs.c:902-904`) ignores SIGINT only after `fork()`. A 100-iteration
+  probe hit it 18/100 on x64 and 12/100 on ARM64.
+
+## Test Fixes Applied
+None. All tests passed with no changes to dash.
+
+## Benchmark Results
+- **Result file**: `benchmarks/base_bench_win_arm.txt` (committed on `arm64-port`, `84c1485`)
+- **Metadata**: `benchmarks/base_bench_win_arm.meta.json`
+- **Format**: txt (median of 5 wall-time runs, all individual runs listed)
+- **Executed on**: native ARM64. The x64 column is the x64 build emulated (Prism) on the same machine.
+
+| Benchmark | Workload | ARM64 native | x64 emulated | ARM64/x64 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| arith_loop | 1e6-iteration `$(( ))` loop | 3474.3 ms | 3156.7 ms | 1.10 |
+| string_build | 200 x build/trim 100-item string | 195.7 ms | 175.5 ms | 1.12 |
+| case_match | 30000 x 10-word case/glob | 536.3 ms | 445.2 ms | 1.20 |
+| func_call | 200000 function calls | 835.3 ms | 838.9 ms | 1.00 |
+| fork_subshell | 500 x `$(:)` | 3068.5 ms | 6243.6 ms | 0.49 |
+
+Fork-heavy work (the normal shape of shell scripts) is about 2x faster native.
+Pure interpreter loops are 0-20% slower than emulated x64. Contributing factors:
+- the cross GCC defaults to `-march=armv8-a -mtune=cortex-a53` (verified with
+  `-Q --help=target`), not an Oryon tuning
+- the runtimes differ (ARM64 msys-2.0.dll 3.6.10 vs x64 3.6.12)
+
+## NEON Optimizations
+Phase 7 skipped: WOS_SKIP_OPTIMIZE set. dash has no SIMD or vectorisable hot
+kernels in any case.
+
+## ARM64 vs x64 Performance Comparison
+No x64 reference benchmark file was supplied, so the differential comparison
+does not apply. The same-machine emulated-x64 numbers are in the Benchmark
+Results table above.
+
+## Architecture Verification
+Verified with `dumpbin /HEADERS` (MSVC 14.51 HostARM64\ARM64):
+
+| Binary | Machine | Size |
+|---|---|---|
+| build-arm64/src/dash.exe | AA64 machine (ARM64) | 163,040 bytes |
+| build-x64/src/dash.exe (reference) | 8664 machine (x64) | 180,272 bytes |
+
+All seven generated sources (`signames.c`, `syntax.c`, `syntax.h`, `nodes.h`,
+`builtins.h`, `token.h`, `init.c`) are byte-identical between the ARM64 and x64
+builds.
+
